{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22806e14\" lines=\"23-37\">\n<description>\nMark the center of each odd-sized solid square with the plus marker color, erasing the marker only when it is outside the marked-center field.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is background.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: one unique 3x3 plus marker and one color forming separated solid square objects.\n\nRule:\n- For every non-marker 4-connected object, assert it is a solid square.\n- If the square side length is odd, recolor its center pixel to the plus marker color; even-sided squares stay unchanged.\n- Preserve the plus marker when its center lies inside the bounding box of the odd-square centers; otherwise erase it to background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Odd-sided solid squares have a single visible center pixel, and the plus supplies the color used to identify those centers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\" lines=\"42-56\">\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4a1cacc2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nFill the rectangle from the lone foreground pixel to its nearest grid corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a uniform background field with exactly one foreground pixel.\n- The foreground pixel's color is the color used for all added pixels.\n\nRule\n- Find the grid corner with the smallest Manhattan distance to the foreground pixel.\n- Recolor every pixel in the inclusive axis-aligned rectangle bounded by that\n  corner and the foreground pixel to the foreground color.\n\nKey insight\n- The single pixel is one corner of the rectangle; the opposite corner is the\n  nearest corner of the whole canvas.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"182e5d0f\" lines=\"89-103\">\n<description>\nMove each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.\n- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.\n\nRule:\n- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.\n- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.\n- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"833966f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSwap the upper two-pixel segment and the lower two-pixel segment around the fixed center pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a 5x1 vertical column of individual colored pixels.\n- The middle pixel is a separator between two same-size two-pixel segments.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the middle pixel in place.\n- Reverse the order of the two pixels above the middle.\n- Reverse the order of the two pixels below the middle.\n\nKey insight:\n- The transformation is local vertical order reversal on each side of the center, not a color-specific rule.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"38007db0\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nExtract the unique cell from each lattice row into a single-column lattice.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into equal square cells by uniform gridlines of color L.\n- Cell interiors filled with bg; each cell has an inner sprite pattern.\n- Each row of cells has exactly one cell with a unique pattern; the rest are copies.\n\nRule:\n- For each lattice row, find the cell whose inner pattern appears exactly once (the \"odd one out\").\n- Construct a single-column lattice preserving the original row heights, containing only the unique cells.\n\nKey insight: The task is per-row \"odd one out\" — every cell in a row is identical except one oddball, which must be extracted.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"800d221b\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor a path-network by adjacent seed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Path color P forms a thin orthogonal network with a solid square hub (NxN).\n- Seed colors appear in colored regions (non-bg, non-P) adjacent to path branches.\n\nRule:\n- Infer P as the non-bg color whose largest component is most network-like.\n- Find the largest solid k×k all-P square (the hub); exclude it from recoloring.\n- Recolor each path branch to the seed-region color it touches most.\n- Recolor the hub center pixel to the seed whose branches touch the hub most.\n\nKey insight: The hub stays uncolored; branches inherit color from adjacent seed regions by adjacency count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17b866bd\" lines=\"49-67\">\n<description>\nMove each marker from a lattice corner gap into the adjacent enclosed 12-pixel cavity.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a repeated teal lattice around black 12-pixel cavities.\n- A marker is a non-black pixel placed at the northwest corner gap of one\n  complete 5x5 lattice tile; the marker may itself be teal.\n\nRule:\n- For every marked northwest corner gap, clear that marker to black.\n- Fill the 12 black pixels in the tile immediately down-right of the marker\n  with the marker color.\n- Leave the rest of the lattice unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The apparent isolated markers are control pixels for the adjacent cavity;\n  teal markers are recognized as extra teal pixels occupying a gap that the\n  periodic lattice template says should be black.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"fc7cae8d\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nAlign a central rectangle to two colored frame-edge controls via rotation/reflection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with two solid-colored border edges (distinct non-bg colors).\n- A central filled rectangle with 1px colored guide edges on two sides, colors matching the frame controls.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate/mirror the object so its guide edges face the same sides as the matching frame controls.\n- Crop off the 1px guide edges from aligned sides.\n\nKey insight: Guide-edge colors encode target frame sides; the correct dihedral transform matches both simultaneously.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ad8a7c0\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nConnect the innermost mirrored marker pair(s) by filling their horizontal span.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Each foreground row contains exactly two foreground pixels mirrored across the vertical center.\n\nRule\n- Find the foreground row pair(s) with the smallest horizontal gap between the two markers.\n- Fill every pixel between those two markers in each selected row with the foreground color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Wider marker pairs are context; the closest marker pair(s) show the unresolved horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5b37cb25\" lines=\"59-73\">\n<description>\nFill each plus-shaped background notch with the color of the frame side it opens toward.\n\nInput structure\n- A one-cell-thick rectangular frame has four solid-colored sides and neutral corner pixels.\n- The frame interior contains a dominant background color and one foreground object color.\n- Some object boundaries contain five-pixel plus-shaped background notches.\n\nRule\n- Find every background plus whose opposite side is supported by foreground object pixels and whose open side faces one frame side.\n- Recolor the five pixels of that plus with the color of the facing frame side.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe frame side colors are direction labels for the open notches in the foreground object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"689c358e\" lines=\"72-88\">\n<description>\nMark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.\n- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
